SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Offset: an edge is a seam of a face only where the face has it twice, and
the intersection of two offsets along an edge of a closed face keeps the
pieces it runs on into (mitcad#2415).

1. A cylinder whose seam lies on the line where a flat side meets it
   tangentially (an arm tangent to a disk, the disk's seam on the tangent
   line: the fuse split the seam there and the piece between the arm's top
   and bottom became the edge between the cylinder and the arm's side),
   shelled with Sharp corners: the thick solid failed ("the offset faces
   do not close up") or, without the disk's rims rounded, built a valid
   solid four times too heavy, at any thickness. That edge keeps both
   curves of the seam on the cylinder though the cylinder's face has it
   once. BRepOffset_Inter2d's SeamRunsOnFromTangentEdge (mitcad#2050) took
   it for a seam (BRep_Tool::IsClosed), found two seams meeting at the
   vertex, and the offset of that vertex was put on the extended offset of
   the tangential edge as an end of it: the edge's offset was not trimmed
   at the offsets of the arm's top and bottom, and the offset of the arm's
   side got no face. A seam is now an edge the face has twice
   (BRepTools::IsReallyClosed).

2. A loft cut to a slot (GitHub #645's design): the offset of the slot's
   round end and of the loft's face below it intersect in three pieces
   meeting at vertices of their own (the enlarged offset of the cylinder
   past the slot's end). BRepOffset_Tool::Inter3D, for a closed reference
   face and a non-elementary offset, kept only the piece nearest the
   edge's middle ("remove excess edges that are out of range"), which
   stopped 4.7 mm before the slot's flat side: the edge's image was never
   cut at that corner, and the offsets of the slot's end and of the loft's
   face got no face (the closed offset failed at any thickness). Where
   that piece does not reach past both ends of the edge (the nearest point
   of its curve to an end of the edge is one of its own ends), it is now
   kept with the pieces it runs on into through their common vertices, if
   they make one simple run (two free ends, no branch) along a curve that
   is not periodic, and only that run is assembled; else the nearest piece
   stays alone as before (a notch's wall cutting a cone's offset along a
   line split by the cone's seam plane, whose nearest piece covers the
   edge: keeping both pieces hung the offset; a foot circle, trimmed by
   patches 2050 and 2383).

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2415.
